"Orbits Near the Lagrange Points | VirtualDemos",
"Orbits Near the Lagrange Points",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §§{#} to {#} (gravitational potential energy, orbits and Kepler’s laws) and §{#} (centripetal force). Rotating frames go beyond the textbook; the Background gives what you need. Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Sun–Earth: JWST at L2; Earth–Moon; Jupiter’s Trojans; Comet meets Jupiter; Horseshoe orbit; Unstable L1.",
"Two masses, \\(m_1\\) and the smaller \\(m_2\\), orbit their center of mass on circles. A test particle, too light to pull on them, moves in their plane. The picture can be drawn in the frame that turns with the two masses (the rotating frame) or centered on \\(m_1\\).",
"The map shows contours of the effective potential, the five Lagrange points (green when stable, red when not), and the zero velocity curve with the region a particle cannot reach shaded.",
"Click empty space to drop a particle at rest, or drag to throw it. In 3D a drag turns the view; switch the drag to throwing (or hold Shift) to throw. Choose a real system or set the mass ratio \\(\\mu = m_2/(m_1 + m_2)\\) yourself.",
"The rail lists each Lagrange point: its position, and either the period of small librations about it (stable) or the time for a small offset to grow by a factor \\(e\\) (unstable). For the newest particle it gives the Jacobi constant \\(C\\), how much \\(C\\) has drifted in the calculation, and the two body orbit about \\(m_1\\) that the particle would follow if \\(m_2\\) vanished: perihelion, aphelion, period and Tisserand’s parameter.",
"Lengths are in units of the separation \\(a\\) and times in units of the period \\(P\\) of the two masses. The orbits are integrated with an adaptive Dormand–Prince method. Real L1 and L2 spacecraft fly three dimensional halo orbits, and real Trojans have tilted, eccentric orbits; both are left out.",
"Record reading stores the system and \\(\\mu\\), the L1 and L2 distances from \\(m_2\\), the L2 growth time, L4’s stability with its libration period or growth time, the time \\(t\\), and for the newest particle you placed (not a preset swarm) \\(C\\), its drift and the two body orbit. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the Data table below; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Find where the five Lagrange points sit and how stable they are as the mass ratio changes, and follow a comet that meets Jupiter to see what its encounters change and what they leave alone.",
"<strong>The rotating frame.</strong> In the frame that turns with the two masses at their angular speed \\(n = 2\\pi/P\\), a particle feels the gravity of both masses and the centrifugal effect, \\(n^2 r\\) outward. Their combined potential energy per unit mass is \\(-\\Omega\\), with (in units \\(a = 1\\), \\(n = 1\\), \\(G(m_1 + m_2) = 1\\))",
"\\[\\Omega = \\tfrac12(x^2 + y^2) + \\frac{1 - \\mu}{r_1} + \\frac{\\mu}{r_2} + \\tfrac12\\mu(1 - \\mu),\\]",
"where \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\) are the distances to the two masses. A moving particle also feels the Coriolis effect, which pushes sideways to its motion and does no work.",
"<strong>The Lagrange points.</strong> At five points the forces in the rotating frame balance, so a particle at rest there stays at rest. Seen from the stars it orbits with period \\(P\\). L1, L2 and L3 lie on the line through the masses; L4 and L5 make equilateral triangles with them. For small \\(\\mu\\), L1 and L2 sit close to \\(m_2\\), at about Hill’s distance",
"\\[r_H = a\\left(\\frac{\\mu}{3}\\right)^{1/3}.\\]",
"<strong>Stability.</strong> L1, L2 and L3 are saddles: a small offset grows by a factor \\(e\\) in a fixed time. For small \\(\\mu\\) the growth time at L1 and L2 tends to \\(P/(2\\pi\\sqrt{1 + 2\\sqrt7}) = 0.0635\\,P\\). L4 and L5 are hilltops of the effective potential, yet the Coriolis effect turns a particle sliding off into a loop: they are stable when \\(\\mu &lt; 0.03852\\) (Routh’s limit). For small \\(\\mu\\) the slow libration about L4 has period",
"\\[P_{\\text{lib}} \\approx P\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{27\\mu}} = 0.385\\,P\\,\\mu^{-1/2}.\\]",
"<strong>The Jacobi constant.</strong> In the rotating frame the energy per unit mass \\(\\tfrac12 v^2 - \\Omega\\) is conserved, since the Coriolis effect does no work. It is usually written as \\(C = 2\\Omega - v^2\\). Where \\(2\\Omega &lt; C\\) the particle would need \\(v^2 &lt; 0\\): it can never go there. For a comet far from Jupiter, \\(C\\) is close to Tisserand’s parameter, \\(T = a_J/a + 2\\sqrt{(a/a_J)(1 - e^2)}\\), computed from the comet’s two body orbit about the Sun (semimajor axis \\(a\\), eccentricity \\(e\\)).",
"Use Hill’s distance to estimate how far L1 and L2 are from Earth in the Sun–Earth system (\\(\\mu = 3.04\\times10^{-6}\\), \\(a = 1.496\\times10^8\\) km). Compare with the Moon’s distance, {#} km.",
"Find the growth time at Sun–Earth L2 in days, from \\(0.0635\\,P\\) with \\(P = 365.26\\) d. If JWST is {#} km off its planned path, how long until the error is {#} km if nothing is done?",
"Estimate the period of small librations of Jupiter’s Trojans about L4 (\\(\\mu = 9.54\\times10^{-4}\\), \\(P = 11.86\\) yr).",
"<strong>The points against the mass ratio.</strong> Set the mass ratio to each of \\(\\mu = 10^{-6}\\), \\(10^{-5}\\), \\(10^{-4}\\), \\(10^{-3}\\), {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#}, and press <strong>Record reading</strong> at each. To set an exact value, click the number beside the <strong>Mass ratio μ</strong> slider (under The two masses), type it ({#} works) and press Enter; the slider alone is too coarse. The Trojan swarm the demo opens with does not matter here. Then press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong>.",
"<strong>A comet meets Jupiter.</strong> Press the <strong>Comet meets Jupiter</strong> setup. It starts running at once, so press <strong>Pause</strong>, then <strong>Reset</strong> to go back to \\(t = 0\\) (the counter on the map reads t = {#} P), and press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Move the <strong>Time speed</strong> slider one notch left, to {#} \\(P\\) per second, and press <strong>Play</strong>. Press <strong>Pause</strong> as near as you can to \\(t = 0.5\\), {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} \\(P\\) on the counter, and press <strong>Record reading</strong> each time ({#} readings in all). Write down, beside the table, the time of each close pass of Jupiter. Then press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: these readings go into a second copy of the table.",
